/// Chapter 2 of the guide, written from md-docs/02-getting-started.md by
/// scripts/site-from-guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page GettingStartedGuide(path: "/docs/guide/getting-started", title: "Getting started", description: "Install wf and the language server, create a first project, run the dev server, and learn what a build produces.", layout: DocsShell("Getting started", chapter: t("guide.chapter", { n: "2" }), blurb: "Install the compiler and your editor's support, make a project, and see what a build writes.", source: "md-docs/02-getting-started.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "install", label: "Install"), Anchor(id: "set-up-your-editor", label: "Set up your editor"), Anchor(id: "a-first-project", label: "A first project"), Anchor(id: "the-smallest-site", label: "The smallest site"), Anchor(id: "what-a-build-produces", label: "What a build produces"), Anchor(id: "the-project-in-brief", label: "The project, in brief")], prevTo: "/docs/guide/introduction", prevLabel: "1. {t("ch.01")}", nextTo: "/docs/guide/tutorial", nextLabel: "3. {t("ch.03")}")) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("By the end of this chapter you will have installed the compiler, set up your editor, built a two-page site, changed it with the dev server watching, and know what every file the build writes is for.")

        Heading("Install", id: "install").h2

        Markdown("WebFluent is one binary, `wf`. Run the line for your machine; each one leaves `wf` on your `PATH`.")

        Heading("macOS and Linux", id: "macos-and-linux").h3

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "curl -sSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/monzeromer-lab/WebFluent/master/install.sh | bash")
            ])

        Markdown("Fetches the binary for your platform from the latest release — no Rust needed. It covers Apple Silicon and Intel Macs and x86_64 Linux.")

        Heading("Windows (PowerShell)", id: "windows-powershell").h3

        CodeBlock(#"irm https://raw.githubusercontent.com/monzeromer-lab/WebFluent/master/install.ps1 | iex"#, title: "powershell")

        Heading("With Rust", id: "with-rust").h3

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "cargo install webfluent")
            ])

        Markdown("Builds from crates.io. This works everywhere Rust does, and it is how to install on Linux arm64, which has no prebuilt binary yet.")

        Heading("Packages", id: "packages").h3

        Markdown("Each [release](https://github.com/monzeromer-lab/WebFluent/releases) also carries a `.deb`, an `.msi` and plain tarballs, for a machine where piping a script to a shell is not an option.")

        Heading("Check it", id: "check-it").h3

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf --version")
            ])

        Markdown("If the shell cannot find `wf`, open a new terminal: the installer adds its directory to your `PATH` in your shell's startup file, which the session you ran it in had already read.\n\nBoth installers read two environment variables: `WF_INSTALL_DIR` puts the binary somewhere other than `~/.webfluent/bin`, and `WF_VERSION` pins a release (`WF_VERSION=v4.0.1`) rather than taking the latest. Running the installer again upgrades in place.\n\nTo build from a clone instead — for an unreleased change, or to work on the compiler itself:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "git clone https://github.com/monzeromer-lab/WebFluent.git"),
                Line(command: "cd WebFluent && cargo install --path .")
            ])

        Heading("Set up your editor", id: "set-up-your-editor").h2

        Markdown("The language server, `wf-lsp`, gives your editor the same checks `wf build` runs, as you type: diagnostics, completion of props, flags and cases, hover with types and docs, go to definition, rename and quick fixes.\n\nIt is a second binary. Get it from the same [release](https://github.com/monzeromer-lab/WebFluent/releases) as `wf` — `wf-lsp-<version>-<arch>-<os>.tar.gz` — and put it on your `PATH`, or build it from a clone:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "cargo install --path crates/wf-lsp")
            ])

        Markdown("- **VS Code** — the extension in `editors/vscode` brings highlighting and the language server. Until it is on the Marketplace, run `npm ci && npm run package` in that folder and install the `.vsix` it writes with *Extensions: Install from VSIX*.\n- **Zed** — the extension in `editors/zed` brings Tree-sitter highlighting, the outline, snippets and the language server (found on `PATH`, or downloaded from the latest release). Install it with *zed: install dev extension* pointed at that folder.\n- **Neovim, Helix, Emacs, anything else with LSP** — run `wf-lsp` over stdio for the `webfluent` file type (`*.wf`, `*.wfx`). [Editors](/docs/guide/cli#editors) has configuration for each.")

        Heading("A first project", id: "a-first-project").h2

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf init hello"),
                Line(command: "cd hello"),
                Line(command: "wf serve")
            ])

        Markdown("`wf init` writes a small application — four pages (a dashboard, a task list, a profile and settings), three components and two stores — so you have something real to read. `wf serve` builds it, serves it at `http://localhost:3000`, rebuilds when you save, reloads the page when a build lands, and — when a build fails — draws the error over the page until the next save fixes it.\n\n`wf init` takes a template:")

        Table(caption: "A first project") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Template").header
                    Table.Cell("Makes").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`-t spa` (default)") }
                    Table.Cell("An interactive application, routed in the browser")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`-t static`") }
                    Table.Cell("A marketing site, every page pre-rendered to HTML")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`-t pdf`") }
                    Table.Cell("A PDF document")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`-t slides`") }
                    Table.Cell("A PDF slide deck")
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("Not sure between `spa` and `static`? Start with `static`: every page is real HTML before any script runs, which is better for search and for a first paint, and it is still fully interactive once the script loads. [Static or single-page](/docs/guide/static-and-spa) explains the difference.")

        Heading("The smallest site", id: "the-smallest-site").h2

        Markdown("Replace `src/` with two files.\n\n`src/App.wf` — the shell every page shares, and the router:")

        CodeBlock(#"app {
    Navbar {
        Navbar.Brand { Text("Hello").heading }
        Navbar.Links {
            Link("Home", to: "/")
            Link("About", to: "/about")
        }
    }
    Router
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("`src/pages/Home.wf` — pages, each owning its route:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Home(path: "/", title: "Hello", description: "A first WebFluent site.") {
    state count = 0
    Container {
        Heading("Hello, world").h1
        Text("You have clicked {count} times.")
        Button("Click me").primary { on click { count = count + 1 } }
    }
}

page About(path: "/about", title: "About", description: "What this site is for.") {
    Container {
        Heading("About").h1
        Paragraph("Two pages, one shell, no framework.")
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("Read it top to bottom:\n\n- `app \{ … \}` is the shell: what every page sits inside. `Router` is where the current page renders. There is no route table — each `page` names its own `path:`.\n- `page Home(path: \"/\", title: …, description: …)` declares a page. `title` and `description` become the `<title>`, the meta description, the Open Graph and Twitter cards and the JSON-LD; leave them out and the build warns you.\n- `state count = 0` is a signal. `Text(\"… \{count\} …\")` reads it, so the text updates when it changes.\n- `Button(\"Click me\").primary \{ on click \{ … \} \}` — the label is the one positional argument, `.primary` is a flag (a case of the button's `tone` prop), and the block holds the click handler.\n- `Container`, `Heading`, `Text`, `Paragraph`, `Navbar` and `Link` are built-ins; the [components reference](/docs/reference) lists all of them.\n\nSave, and the dev server shows the change. Run `wf build` to write the finished site to `build/`.")

        Heading("What a build produces", id: "what-a-build-produces").h2

        CodeBlock(#"build/
  index.html          the page, pre-rendered (static) or the app shell (spa)
  about/index.html    one directory per route in a static build
  404.html            the `path: "*"` page, which a host serves for unknown paths
  app.js              the runtime this program needs, its stores and components
  pages/Home.js       each page in its own chunk, loaded when its route shows
  pages/Home.css      the styles only that page reaches
  styles.css          tokens, the built-ins you used, your stylesheets
  sitemap.xml         every indexable route (needs meta.site_url)
  robots.txt
  _headers            the security headers (build.csp), for hosts that read the file
  sw.js               the service worker, when the config names "offline"
  *.gz                pre-compressed copies a host can serve as they are"#, title: "text")

        Markdown("Nothing in `build/` is for editing: it is written again on every build, so keep it out of version control. `public/` is copied to its root as it is.\n\n`app.js` carries only the parts of the runtime the program reaches. The runtime is a set of feature modules — `each` for a `for`, `router` for a second page, `net` for a `resource`, `icons` for an `Icon` — and the build works out which ones the code it just wrote calls. A page of static text carries 3 of its 37 modules — an `app.js` of 3.7 kB gzipped. `wf build --stats` shows what a build weighs; [Performance](/docs/guide/performance) reads it.")

        Heading("The project, in brief", id: "the-project-in-brief").h2

        CodeBlock(#"webfluent.app.json    the config
src/
  App.wf              the app shell (optional)
  pages/*.wf          pages
  components/*.wf     components
  stores/*.wf         stores
  *.css               your own stylesheets, bundled into styles.css
  translations/*.json translation files, with "i18n" in the config
public/               copied to the build's root (images, fonts, favicon)
tests/*.wf            `test "…" { }` declarations for `wf test`"#, title: "text")

        Markdown("Every `.wf` or `.wfx` file under `src/` is read and merged into one program: a page in one file uses a component from another without importing it. The folders are convention, not rules. [Project structure](/docs/guide/project-structure) covers naming, organisation and the config; the [configuration reference](/docs/guide/config) lists every key.\n\nThe config needs only a name:")

        CodeBlock(#"{ "name": "hello" }"#, title: "json")
    }
}
